#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:16
msgid "Abstract"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:18
msgid ""
"In early versions of Python all namespaces, whether in functions, classes or "
"modules, were all implemented the same way: as a dictionary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:21
msgid ""
"For performance reasons, the implementation of function namespaces was "
"changed. Unfortunately this meant that accessing these namespaces through "
"``locals()`` and ``frame.f_locals`` ceased to be consistent and some odd "
"bugs crept in over the years as threads, generators and coroutines were "
"added."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:27
msgid ""
"This PEP proposes making these namespaces consistent once more. "
"Modifications to ``frame.f_locals`` will always be visible in the underlying "
"variables. Modifications to local variables will immediately be visible in "
"``frame.f_locals``, and they will be consistent regardless of threading or "
"coroutines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:33
msgid ""
"The ``locals()`` function will act the same as it does now for class and "
"modules scopes. For function scopes it will return an instantaneous snapshot "
"of the underlying ``frame.f_locals`` rather than implicitly refreshing a "
"single shared dictionary cached on the frame object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:41
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:43
msgid ""
"The implementation of ``locals()`` and ``frame.f_locals`` in releases up to "
"and including Python 3.12 is slow, inconsistent and buggy. We want to make "
"it faster, consistent, and most importantly fix the bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:47
msgid ""
"For example, when attempting to manipulate local variables via frame "
"objects::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:54
msgid "prints ``2``, but::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:62
msgid "prints ``1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:64
msgid ""
"This is inconsistent, and confusing. Worse than that, the Python 3.12 "
"behavior can result in strange `bugs <https://github.com/python/cpython/"
"issues/74929>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:67
msgid ""
"With this PEP both examples would print ``2`` as the function level change "
"would be written directly to the optimized local variables in the frame "
"rather than to a cached dictionary snapshot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:71
msgid ""
"There are no compensating advantages for the Python 3.12 behavior; it is "
"unreliable and slow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:74
msgid ""
"The ``locals()`` builtin has its own undesirable behaviours. Refer to :pep:"
"`558` for additional details on those concerns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:81
msgid "Rationale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:84
msgid "Making the ``frame.f_locals`` attribute a write-through proxy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:86
msgid ""
"The Python 3.12 implementation of ``frame.f_locals`` returns a dictionary "
"that is created on the fly from the array of local variables. The "
"``PyFrame_LocalsToFast()`` C API is then called by debuggers and trace "
"functions that want to write their changes back to the array (until Python "
"3.11, this API was called implicitly after every trace function invocation "
"rather than being called explicitly by the trace functions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:93
msgid ""
"This can result in the array and dictionary getting out of sync with each "
"other. Writes to the ``f_locals`` frame attribute may not show up as "
"modifications to local variables if ``PyFrame_LocalsToFast()`` is never "
"called. Writes to local variables can get lost if a dictionary snapshot "
"created before the variables were modified is written back to the frame "
"(since *every* known variable stored in the snapshot is written back to the "
"frame, even if the value stored on the frame had changed since the snapshot "
"was taken)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:102
msgid ""
"By making ``frame.f_locals`` return a view on the underlying frame, these "
"problems go away. ``frame.f_locals`` is always in sync with the frame "
"because it is a view of it, not a copy of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:107
msgid "Making the ``locals()`` builtin return independent snapshots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:109
msgid ""
":pep:`558` considered three potential options for standardising the behavior "
"of the ``locals()`` builtin in :term:`optimized scopes <py3.13:optimized "
"scope>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:112
msgid ""
"retain the historical behaviour of having each call to ``locals()`` on a "
"given frame update a single shared snapshot of the local variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:114
msgid ""
"make ``locals()`` return write-through proxy instances (similar to ``frame."
"f_locals``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:116
msgid ""
"make ``locals()`` return genuinely independent snapshots so that attempts to "
"change the values of local variables via ``exec()`` would be *consistently* "
"ignored rather than being accepted in some circumstances"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:120
msgid ""
"The last option was chosen as the one which could most easily be explained "
"in the language reference, and memorised by users:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:123
msgid ""
"the ``locals()`` builtin gives an instantaneous snapshot of the local "
"variables in optimized scopes, and read/write access in other scopes; and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:125
msgid ""
"``frame.f_locals`` gives read/write access to the local variables in all "
"scopes, including optimized scopes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:128
msgid ""
"This approach allows the intent of a piece of code to be clearer than it "
"would be if both APIs granted full read/write access in optimized scopes, "
"even when write access wasn't needed or desired. For additional details on "
"this design decision, refer to :pep:`558`, especially the :ref:`pep-558-"
"motivation` section and :ref:`pep-558-exec-eval-impact`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:133
msgid ""
"This approach is not without its drawbacks, which are covered in the "
"Backwards Compatibility section below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:137
msgid "Specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:140
msgid "Python API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:145
msgid "The ``frame.f_locals`` attribute"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:147
msgid ""
"For module and class scopes (including ``exec()`` and ``eval()`` "
"invocations), ``frame.f_locals`` is a direct reference to the local variable "
"namespace used in code execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:151
msgid ""
"For function scopes (and other :term:`optimized scopes <py3.13:optimized "
"scope>`) it will be an instance of a new write-through proxy type that can "
"directly modify the optimized local variable storage array in the underlying "
"frame, as well as the contents of any cell references to non-local variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:156
msgid ""
"The view objects fully implement the ``collections.abc.Mapping`` interface, "
"and also implement the following mutable mapping operations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:159
msgid "using assignment to add new key/value pairs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:160
msgid "using assignment to update the value associated with a key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:161
msgid "conditional assignment via the ``setdefault()`` method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:162
msgid "bulk updates via the ``update()`` method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:164
msgid ""
"Views of different frames compare unequal even if they have the same "
"contents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:166
msgid ""
"All writes to the ``f_locals`` mapping will be immediately visible in the "
"underlying variables. All changes to the underlying variables will be "
"immediately visible in the mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:170
msgid ""
"The ``f_locals`` object will be a full mapping, and can have arbitrary key-"
"value pairs added to it. New names added via the proxies will be stored in a "
"dedicated shared dictionary stored on the underlying frame object (so all "
"proxy instances for a given frame will be able to access any names added "
"this way)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:176
msgid ""
"Extra keys (which do not correspond to local variables on the underlying "
"frame) may be removed as usual with ``del`` statements or the ``pop()`` "
"method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:180
msgid ""
"Using ``del``, or the ``pop()`` method, to remove keys that correspond to "
"local variables on the underlying frame is NOT supported, and attempting to "
"do so will raise ``ValueError``. Local variables can only be set to ``None`` "
"(or some other value) via the proxy, they cannot be unbound completely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:186
msgid ""
"The ``clear()`` method is NOT implemented on the write-through proxies, as "
"it is unclear how it should handle the inability to delete entries "
"corresponding to local variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:190
msgid ""
"To maintain backwards compatibility, proxy APIs that need to produce a new "
"mapping (such as ``copy()``) will produce regular builtin ``dict`` "
"instances, rather than write-through proxy instances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../peps/peps/pep-0667.rst:194
msgid ""
"To avoid introducing a circular reference between frame objects and the "
"write-through proxies, each access to ``frame.f_locals`` returns a *new* "
"write-through proxy instance."
msgstr ""

